\begin{shaded}

\section*{Summary} A retrospective analysis of the Pember Fan monitoring data indicates that the species composition of the fan is changing. Grasses (both exotic and indigenous) are increasing in areal coverage while forbs are diminishing. At the same time the amount of bare ground is shrinking. There are also indications the fan is becoming more depauperate with indigenous biodiversity decreasing over time. \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical), an indigenous forb restricted to the southern half of the fan, is disappearing quickly, and global extinction appears imminent if its current trajectory continues.  As evidenced by this retrospective analysis, the current monitoring regime has proven sufficient for detecting changes in  community composition and areal coverage of the species of primary concern.  


\end{shaded}


\section*{Background}

The Pember Fan is an alluvial outwash surface in Lees Valley, North Canterbury, formed from gravels deposited where the Ashley/Rakahuri River emerges into the valley \citep{newell}. Much of the area is classified as a 'threatened land environment’. The fan supports a grassland which includes several nationally threatened and at-risk plants, most notably the nationally critical Lees Valley daisy (\emph{Brachyscome pinnata}), whose only known natural population occurs there and whose seeds, unlike most members of the Asteraceae family, are not wind dispersed \citep{newell}. Other threatened species known to inhabit the fan include \emph{Gingidia enysii}, \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} and \emph{Raoulia monroi}\footnote{Not present in the transect data}.

In 2018 the Department of Conservation established a monitoring programme based on 30 transects\footnote{Called Plots in the parent data set.} (each with 50 subplots) to monitor vegetative changes in this ecosystem through time \citep{newell}. The intended purpose of the monitoring was to assess changes in the site’s conservation values to inform ongoing management. Of particular interest were changes in the abundance of threatened native species especially \emph{Brachyscome pinnata}, changes in native and exotic vegetation, and weed occurrence.  Collectively, this information would be used by DOC to offer guidance on the frequency and intensity of grazing (in order to promote native species diversity and conservation).

Key metrics focused on changes in:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Indigenous biodiversity
  \item Plant composition 
  \item Ground cover (especially threatened species) 
\end{itemize}

The 30 transects which make up the monitoring programme consist of two types: (1)  ten closely spaced transects restricted to a recreation reserve, and (2) twenty more widely spaced transects found across a planned conservation covenant (see Appendix Figure \ref{fig:signal.corrected} for layout).

Importantly, as quantitative data on the grazing intensity has not been kept and there has been a switch from farming sheep to farming cattle this metric cannot be analytically investigated.



\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
     \includegraphics[width=12cm,keepaspectratio]{"Pember fan layout.png"}
     \caption{General layout of the Pember Fan transects in relation to the Ashley/Rakahuri River. Key: red line = fence line, green points = transects in planned covenant, pink points = transects in recreation reserve. }
     \label{fig:distance.fence}
     
\end{figure}




\section*{Purpose of current analysis}

It is hypothesised that the current grazing regime is insufficient to regulate the crowding out of native herbs by exotic grasses on the Pember Fan. Going forward it was agreed that a two-stage analysis of the data would take place:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stage 1:} Would consist of a series of retrospective analyses to understand the trajectories at the site in respect of the three metrics previously identified. The deliverables were to include a short report that would inform a discussion within DOC regarding the direction of the next stage [this document].
  \item \textbf{Stage 2:} Based on the decisions undertaken at the end of Stage 1, the signals present in the data, and operational constraints, a series of statistical tools would be employed to help design an experimental study to understand the effectiveness of any future management intervention (not covered here).
  
\end{itemize}

\textbf{This report represents the culmination of Stage 1.}
  

\section*{Analysis}

Statistical analysis began with some simple proof-of-concept analyses involving indigenous biodiversity and sequentially introduced complexity as contextual understanding grew. Indigenous biodiversity was examined using generalised linear mixed-effects models (GLMMs), species composition was approached visually, while bare ground and species coverage were assessed using cumulative link models (CLMMs).

All analyses were undertaken in program R version 4.4.2 \citep{rcode}, using a series of packages for increased functionality including: `AICcmodavg' \citep{AICcmodavg}, `glmmTMB' \citep{glmmTMB}, `ordinal' \citep{ordinal}, `sf' \citep{sf}, and the `tidyverse' \citep{tidyverse}. An information-theoretic approach (\emph{sensu} \citealt{burnham}) was used to rank candidate models in terms of parsimony (see Appendix for relevant model selection tables).


\subsection*{Indigenous biodiversity}

Initial exploratory modelling focused on indigenous biodiversity due to its high tangibility and suggested the area is becoming more depauperate over time (see Appendix for details). When more complex analyses were undertaken incorporating spatial elements it was revealed  that while the transects across the covenant area plots (CA) were well dispersed across the fan, the recreation reserve plots (RR) were tightly clustered within a reserve found on the southern edge of the fan but within the wider CA extent. Such tight spatial clustering typically presents a problem in terms of sampling bias and raises issues of spatial autocorrelation. Formal tests of the random errors via Moran's I test failed to confirm that there was some form of spatial autocorrelation\footnote{At the suggestion of Ellen Cieraad I implemented a series of models which examined whether the response variable was associated with a decaying distance relationship with neighbouring observations i.e. via an \emph{exp(pos + 0 $\mid$ spatial\_group)} however these failed to detect a spatial effect}. 

Plotting the random errors associated with the top-ranked model suggested a spatial relationship running perpendicular to the Ashley River (Figure \ref{fig:spatial.signal}). Richard Clayton's suggestion that the relationship might be better described by the relationship with the fence line was then tested. Indeed, modelling revealed that there was likely some gradient influencing the biodiversity response associated with distance from the fence line (Figure \ref{fig:signal.corrected}). Inclusion of this term resulted in smaller random effects. It should be noted that one transect (CA12) falls behind the existing fence line and that this variable likely represents an association with a general east-west gradient rather than the fence line physically having some meaningful causal effect.

The predictions from the new top-ranked model (\emph{Year + scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot)}, AICc weight = 0.72, see Appendix for details) suggested that samples found closer to the fence line had higher indigenous biodiversity but all plots lost biodiversity over time at an equivalent rate (Figure \ref{fig:distance.fence}).



\begin{landscape}
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
     \includegraphics[width=18cm,keepaspectratio]{"Pember Fan distance from fence.png"}
     \caption{The top-ranked biodiversity model predicted samples closer to the fence line had higher initial indigenous biodiversity but lost indigenous species at the same rate as those located further away. Note: model predictions are shown on the real scale.}
     \label{fig:distance.fence}
     
\end{figure}
\end{landscape}

\subsection*{Bare ground cover}

Analyses of bare ground were conducted as cover classes via cumulative link mixed-effect models (which account for the non-independence of the subplots). Bare ground was also graphically assessed by transforming cover classes into approximate proportions at the subplot level (based on the mid-point of the Braun–Blanquet scale). While other cover classes on the fan appear to be stable, it is evident that bare ground appears to be disappearing regardless of metric (Figures \ref{fig:barecoverclass}, \ref{fig:bare}, \& \ref{fig:bgcover}). The top-ranked model was \emph{as.factor(Year) + (1 $\mid$ Plot) + (1 $\mid$ SubplotID)} (AICc weight = 0.57). The predictions from this model (see Appendix Figure \ref{fig:barepred}) closely align with those observed (Figure \ref{fig:barecoverclass}). The second-ranked model replicated the variables of the first but with the inclusion of distance from fence, and indicated bare ground increased with distance from the fence (AICc weight = 0.43). The stronger support for \emph{Year} as a factor rather than as a continuous variable suggests that bare ground cover varies year-to-year in a more idiosyncratic manner rather than simply a linear trend over time.


