\subsection*{Plant composition}

Plant composition was analysed graphically; represented as the aggregated proportion of species recorded as present across all subplots. Collectively, the species composition was dominated by exotic plants (Figure \ref{fig:TaxonBiostatus}).

Over time there was a trend towards increasing dominance of species with graminoid (grass-like) growth forms and away from forbs (Figure \ref{fig:TaxonGrowthForm}). This appears to be a generalised trend, with a similar pattern occurring on transects inside the recreation reserve and planned covenant area (Figures \ref{fig:bytransforbs} \& \ref{fig:bytransgrass}). It is not only exotic grasses which appear to be increasingly dominating the Pember Fan but indigenous grasses as well, while at the same time the coverage by indigenous forbs is declining (Figure \ref{fig:bytransgrass}). While there are some differences between transect types the composition of the grass species seems to be relatively stable,  dominated by \emph{Agrostis capillaris}, \emph{Anthoxanthum odoratum} and \emph{Rytidosperma clavatum}. Both transect types document the gradual disappearance of \emph{Vulpia bromoides}, a species often associated with disturbance (Figure  \ref{fig:top8grass}). The appearance of \emph{Aira caryophyllea} appears to be periodic and largely restricted to the recreation reserve (Figure  \ref{fig:top8grass}). In contrast, the forb community appears to be dominated by the hawkweed \emph{Pilosella officinarum} (Figure \ref{fig:top8forb}).

