\appendix


\section*{Conclusions}

As evidenced by this retrospective analysis, the current monitoring regime appears adequate for assessing community and population trajectories on the Pember Fan and for this reason should be continued without change. The trajectories found in the data set align with the observations and assessments of DOC staff. Grasses (both exotic and indigenous) are increasing in areal coverage. At the same time the amount of bare ground is shrinking and there are indications the fan is becoming more depauperate over time. \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical), an indigenous forb restricted to the southern half of the fan, is disappearing quickly, and global extinction appears imminent if its current trajectory continues.  In light of current management constraints, an experimental approach in terms of grazing has been assessed as impractical. However, this should not preclude the development of alternative experimental designs assessing the effect of grass control.


