\subsection*{Initial biodiversity analysis}

When indigenous biodiversity is only assessed at the plot level\footnote{treating each subplot as a replicate}, a pattern (top-ranked model: \mytilde \emph{Year * Exotic + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AIC weight = 0.85) emerges in which indigenous biodiversity appears to reduce over time along with exotic biodiversity (Figure \ref{fig:simpl.biodiversity}). This model was found to have good predictive agreement, thereby suggesting the community might be becoming more depauperate over time. However, upon discussion the pattern was regarded as potentially spurious due to the sensitivity of the plot-level metric to chance, non-representative species occurrences it was determined that a nested plot-subplot approach would be a fairer test given the study design (see Indigenous biodiversity section in the report).

During the exploratory process I identified that there was meaningful underdispersion (i.e the variance in biodiversity results was less than expected). Use of the generalised Poisson distribution family improved diagnostics but the use of a Conway-Maxwell Poisson distribution was generally better. However, the Conway-Maxwell Poisson distribution proved to have a massive impact on computability (with inclusion of subplot level random effects causing such slowness it effectively negated any practical usage). Given that the coefficients produced by generalised Poisson and Conway-Maxwell Poisson distributions were generally very similar to each other, I was advised to use the generalised Poisson.


